## Why This Is Asked
This probes cross-region real-time reliability, backpressure handling, and semantics trade-offs in a live data plane.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency monitoring; regional failover
- Backpressure and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Offset management and idempotent processing
- Chaos testing and post-incident reviews

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo idempotent replay handler
function processEvent(evt){
  const id = evt.id;
  if (processedSet.has(id)) return;
  store.update(evt.payload);
  processedSet.add(id);
  commitOffsets(evt.offset);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this plan with simulated regional outages?
- What telemetry would you add to quantify readiness and bottlenecks?

## Why This Is Asked

Telemetry quality is critical for root cause analysis and cross-service tracing; this checks practical skills for CI, instrumentation, and rollback safety.

## Key Concepts

- Telemetry integrity, trace propagation, OpenTelemetry, CI gates, canary deployments
- Error budgets and dashboards alignment
- Synthetic validation and fallback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// OpenTelemetry middleware for trace propagation
const { trace, context, propagation } = require('@opentelemetry/api');

function ensureTraceId(req, res, next) {
  const carrier = propagation.createExtractHeadersCarrier(req.headers);
  const ctx = propagation.extract(context.active(), carrier);

  if (!trace.getSpan(ctx).spanContext().traceId) {
    const tracer = trace.getTracer('default');
    const span = tracer.startSpan('fallback-trace');
    propagation.inject(context.active(), carrier);
  }
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure telemetry coverage and what would trigger a rollback?
- What trade-offs exist when dropping events missing trace_id?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-region consistency, edge-scale reliability, and clear runbooks for failures.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed consensus with multi-DC Raft and quorum tuning
- Partition tolerance and per-tenant sharding
- Idempotent, sequenced operations
- Anti-entropy reconciliation and safe replay
- Observability, alerting, and rollback runbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// simple idempotent write with sequence tokens
function applyWrite(store, key, value, token) {
  const cur = store[key];
  if (!cur || token > cur.token) {
    store[key] = { value, token };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the reconciliation path under partitions?
- What metrics signal reconciliation backlog and potential data loss?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes hands-on ability to design and automate incident response with real-world constraints (Kubernetes, Kafka, Istio). It tests metric selection, safe auto-remediation, and rollback discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Metrics: p95 latency, error rate, Kafka lag, cache metrics
- Auto-remediation: rate limiting, circuit breakers, HPA, Kafka backpressure
- Feature flags and canaries for safe rollback
- Validation window and explicit rollback criteria
- Safety and regional considerations in a live prod system

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple heuristic for rollback decision
function needsRollback(baseline, current) {
  const latencySpike = current.p95Latency > baseline.p95Latency * 1.2;
  const errorSpike = current.errorRate > baseline.errorRate * 1.5;
  const lagExcess = current.kafkaLag > baseline.kafkaLag * 1.4;
  return latencySpike || errorSpike || lagExcess;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust thresholds per region or per hour of the day?
- How do you safely simulate this in staging without impacting real users?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesss real-world ability to design resilient, low-tail-latency multi-region pipelines under stress while preserving tenant fairness and clear rollback paths.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and circuit breaking in streaming pipelines
- Per-tenant QoS and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once processing guarantees across Kafka and sinks
- Observability for SLO-driven tail latency
- Chaos testing and incident rollback discipline

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bounded per-tenant backpressure
function handleEvent(event, tenant) {
  const q = queues[tenant];
  if (q.length < MAX_PER_TENANT) {
    enqueue(event, tenant);
  } else {
    drop(event); // apply backpressure upstream
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation plan with synthetic workloads?
- What metrics would you use to decide when to restore normal traffic?
